<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>1.5. Green's Theorem</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 401</alert>
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-Greens-theorem">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <term>Green's Theorem: </term> For a two-dimensional field
                <m>\mbf F = (P,Q)</m> and a closed curve <m>C</m> that is
                oriented counterclockwise (positively oriented) and that
                encloses the region <m>D</m>, the work done by <m>\mbf F</m>
                along <m>C</m> is
                <md>
                    \ds \oint_C \mbf F \cdot ~d\mbf r = \iint_D  \left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right)~dA = \iint_D \nabla \times \mbf F~dA
                </md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use Green's Theorem to evaluate the line integral
                <m>\ds \oint_C -y~dx + x~dy</m> where <m>C</m> is the unit
                circle oriented in the counterclockwise direction.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="S1-5-soln">
            <p>
              We have <m>P=-y, \ Q = x</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} = -1</m> and 
              <m>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} = 1</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=1-(-1)=2</m>
              <md>
                \oint_C -y~dx + x~dy = \iint_D 2dA = 2(area \ of \ D) = 2\pi.
              </md>
              
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate the line integral <m>\ds \oint_C y^2~dx + 3xy~dy</m>
                where <m>C</m> is the curve shown below.
                You may want to integrate in polar coordinates.
            </p>

            <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                <shortdescription>
                    A closed curve, see description below.
                </shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve1">
                    <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
                        <coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-1.5,2.5,3.5)">
                            <grid-axes spacings="((-2,0.5,2),(-1,0.5,3))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                            <path start="(1,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
                                <lineto point="(2,0)"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="2" range="(0,180)"/>
                                <lineto point="(-1,0)"/>
                                <arc center="(0,0)" radius="1" range="(180,0)"/>
                                \
                            </path>
                            <label anchor="(1.5,1.5)" alignment="n" offset="(0,5)">
                                <m>
                                    C
                                </m>
                            </label>
                        </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                </prefigure>

                <description>
                    <p>
                        A closed curve <m>C</m> that starts at <m>(1,0)</m>,
                        moves in a straight line to <m>(2,0)</m>, continues in a
                        half circle centered at the origin with radius <m>2</m>
                        in the upper half plane until the point <m>(-2,0)</m>,
                        then goes in a straight line to the point <m>(-1,0)</m>,
                        and then traverses the half circle centered at the
                        origin with a radius of 1 located in upper half plane to
                        end back at the point <m>(1,0)</m>.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </statement>

        <solution component="S1-5-soln">
            <p>
              We have <m>P=y^2, \ Q = 3xy</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} = 2y</m> and 
              <m>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} = 3y</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=3y-2y=y</m>, so then
              <md>
                \oint_C y^2~dx + 3xy~dy = \iint_D y dA.
              </md>
              Let's use polar coordinates, <m>x=r\cos{\theta}, \ y=r\sin{\theta}</m>, and 
              <m>dA = r dr d\theta</m> where <m>{1\le r \le 2}</m> and 
              <m>{0\le \theta \le \pi}</m>.  Plugging everything in to the integral:
              <md>
                <mrow>\iint_D y dA \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\pi}\int_{r=1}^2 r\sin{\theta} r dr d\theta </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\pi} \left. \frac13 r^3 \right|_{1}^2 \sin{\theta} d\theta</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\pi} \frac73\sin\theta d\theta </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \left. -\frac73 \cos\theta \right|_{0}^{\pi} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \frac{14}{3}</mrow>
              </md>
              
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>\ds \mbf F= (xy, \arctan y)</m> and <m>C</m> is
                the simple closed curve shown below.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the line integral <m>\owork{C}{F}</m>.
                </p>

                <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A closed curve, see description below.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve2">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                                <grid-axes spacings="((-4,1,4),(-4,1,4))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                                <path start="(3,0)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
                                    <arc center="(0,0)" radius="3" range="(0,180)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(3,0)"/>
                                    \
                                </path>
                                <label anchor="(2,2)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,5)">
                                    <m>
                                        C
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A closed curve <m>C</m> that starts at <m>(3,0)</m>,
                            continues in a half circle centered at the origin
                            with radius <m>3</m> in the upper half plane until
                            the point <m>(-3,0)</m>, then returns in a straight
                            line along the x-axis to the point <m>(3,0)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>

            <solution component="S1-5-soln">
                <p>
                  We have <m>P=xy, \ Q = \arctan{y}</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} = x</m> and 
              <m>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} = 0</m>, so <m>\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=0-x = -x.</m>
              Then <m>\owork{C}{F} = \iint_D -x dA</m>.  Polar coordinates would be good:
              <m>x=r\cos{\theta}, \ y=r\sin{\theta}</m>, and 
              <m>dA = r dr d\theta</m> where <m>{0\le r \le 3}</m> and 
              <m>{0\le \theta \le \pi}</m>.  Plugging everything in to the integral:
              <md>
                <mrow>\owork{C}{F} \amp =\int_{\theta=0}^{\pi} \int_{r=0}^3 -r\cos\theta r dr d\theta</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\pi} \left. -\frac13 r^3 \right|_{r=0}^3 \cos\theta d \theta</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \int_{\theta=0}^{\pi} -9\cos\theta d\theta</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \left. -9\sin\theta\right|_0^{\pi} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = 0. </mrow>
              </md>
              
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use your result to find the line integral
                    <m>\ds \work{C'}{F}</m> where <m>C'</m> is just the upper
                    semi-circle of radius 3 without the straight line along the
                    x-axis.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="S1-5-soln">
                <p>
                  The line is easier, since <m>y=0</m> on the line then the 
                  field simplifies to
                  <m>\mbf F = (0,0) </m>, so <m>\lineint{line}{F} = 0</m>.  Since 
                  the total work for the closed curve is 0, and the work over the line is 0, 
                  then the work over the part we want is the difference of those two. But that's <m>0-0=0</m>. 
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Each of the fields below has a constant curl.
                For each, state whether the curl is negative, zero, or positive.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 25%">
                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all vectors point radially away from the
                        origin.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve3">
                        <diagram dimensions="(180,180)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (x,y)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <vector-field function="f"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all arrows points counter-clockwise in a
                        co-centric circles centered around the origin.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve4">
                        <diagram dimensions="(180,180)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (-y,x)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <vector-field function="f"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

                <image>
                    <shortdescription>
                        A vector field, all arrows point point horizontally
                        towards the y-axis.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve5">
                        <diagram dimensions="(180,180)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                f(x,y) = (-x,0)
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,4]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <vector-field function="f" thickness="2"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>
        <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        Use Green's Theorem here, so the curl is related to the work done around a closed curve.
        
      </p>
      <p>
        First picture. Think of a circle centered at the origin that goes counterclockwise. 
        Then <m>\mbf F</m> is always orthogonal to <m>d\mbf r</m>, so their dot product 
        is always 0, so Green's Theorem then implies the curl <m>\nabla \times \mbf F = \mbf 0</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Second picture. Think of a circle centered at the origin that goes counterclockwise. 
        Then <m>\mbf F</m> is always in the same direction as <m>d\mbf r</m>, so the work
        is positive, and thus the curl is positive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Third picture.  Think of a rectangle centered at the original and oriented up and down. 
        Then on the left and right sides <m>\mbf F</m> is always orthogonal to <m>d\mbf r</m>, and 
        on the top and bottom sides the work cancels out, so the work is 0 which 
        implies the curl is 0.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's make some connections.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.01in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch the vector field <m>\mbf F(x,y) = (y,0)</m> below.
                    (Ignore the curve already drawn for now.)
                </p>

                <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A closed curve on a graph, see description below.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve6">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <path start="(1,1)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
                                    <lineto point="(1,-1)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(3,-1)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(3,1)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(1,1)"/>
                                    
                                </path>
                                <label anchor="(2,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        C
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A grid to sketch a vector field on.
                            Also included is a closed curve <m>C</m> in the
                            shape of a square oriented counterclockwise through
                            the points <m>(1,1), (1,-1), (3,-1), (3,1)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <image width="30%">
        <shortdescription>The vector field points only in the x-direction, but has magnitude y.</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-6-curve6-soln">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="0">
                    <definition> f(x,y) = (y,0)</definition>

                            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
                                <path start="(1,1)" stroke="blue" arrows="1" thickness="4">
                                    <lineto point="(1,-1)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(3,-1)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(3,1)"/>
                                    <lineto point="(1,1)"/>
                                    
                                </path>
                                <label anchor="(2,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,3)">
                                    <m>
                                        C
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <vector-field function="f" stroke="red"/>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        Note the field gets larger in magnitude as you move away from the x-axis.
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Judging from your sketch, is the circulation (the work)
                    around the curve positive or negative?
                    Explain your thinking.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        Think Green's Theorem, <m> \ds \oint_C \mbf F \cdot ~d\mbf r = \iint_D \nabla \times \mbf F~dA</m>, 
        but along the sides of the square we are moving orthogonal to the field, 
        and on the top and bottom of the square we are moving against the field, 
        so on the top and bottom we have <m>\mbf F \cdot d\mbf r \lt 0</m>. Since 
        the work is negative, then the curl is also negative, so the circulation
        around the curve is negative.
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What about if the curve were shifted vertically?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        The top field is stronger than the bottom, so even if the 
        bottom has positive work, the top will have negative work and 
        overpower the bottom, so we will still have negative work and 
        thus negative curl.
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> and explain how the curl
                    is connected to the response to the previous two questions.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        <m>\nabla\times\mbf F =\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=-1 </m>, 
        so we'll actually end up with 
        <md>
          \oint_C \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \iint_D -1 dA = -1(area \ of \ domain \ bounded \ by \ C)
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the field
                <m>\ds \mbf F(x,y) = \frac12\left(-y,x\right)</m> and suppose
                that <m>C</m> is a closed curve bounding a region <m>D</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why <m>\ds \oint_C \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r</m> measures
                    the area of <m>D</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        <md>\oint_C \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r = \iint_D\left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right)dA 
          = \iint_D (\frac12 - (-\frac12))dA = \iint_D 1 dA = (area \ of \ D)
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify this is the case by evaluating the line integral
                    directly where <m>C</m> is the circle of radius <m>R</m>
                    centered at the origin.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        Have to parameterize here!  And not use Green's Theorem. So we'll
        take <md>x = R\cos{t}, y=r\sin{t},</md> then 
        <m>{dx = -R\sin{t}~dt}</m>, and <m>{dy = R\cos{t}~dt}</m>, where 
        <m>t\rightarrow 0 \rightarrow 2\pi</m> is the only variable and <m>R</m> is 
        a constant. So the integral becomes
        <md>
          <mrow> \oint_C \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r \amp = \oint_C -\frac12 y ~dx + \frac12 x~dy </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \int_{0}^{2\pi} -\frac12(R\sin t)(-R\sin t)~dt + \frac12(R\cos t)(R\cos t)~dt </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \int_0^{2\pi} \frac12 R^2 \sin^2 t + \frac12 R^2 \cos^2 t ~dt </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =\int_0^{2\pi} \frac12 R^2 ~dt</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \frac12 R^2 (2\pi) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \pi R^2.</mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Closed curves and a field with zero curl.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> are two paths that
                    begin at the same point and end at the same point and that
                    <m>\nabla\times \mbf F = 0</m> in the region in between
                    <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m>.
                    Explain why
                    <m>\ds \int_{C_1}
  \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r = \int_{C_2} \mbf F\cdot d\mbf r</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        Since they begin and end at the same point, then we can travel around 
        <m>C_1</m> and then backwards along <m>C_2</m> and have a closed curve.
        Then according to Green's Theorem
        <md>
          \oint_{C_1 - C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \iint_D \nabla \times \mbf F dA = 0
        </md>
        since the curl is 0 inside this region.  Then we can break apart 
        <md>
          \oint_{C_1 - C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \oint_{C_1} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r - \oint_{C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r
        </md>
        so setting it equal to 0 gives <md>
          \oint_{C_1} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \oint_{C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>C</m> is a closed curve and
                    <m>\nabla \times \mbf F = 0</m> in the region <m>D</m>
                    bounded by <m>C</m>, explain why
                    <m>\ds \oint_C \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r =
  0</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component = "S1-5-soln">
      <p>
        By Green's Theorem!
        <md>
          \oint_{C} \mbf F \cdot d\mbf r = \iint_D \nabla \times \mbf F dA = \iint_D 0 dA = 0.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>